WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.050
Online safety is not about
being afraid of your computer.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:08.950
It is a set of small decisions
you already know how to make.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:13.841
You would not hand your house key to
a stranger who knocked on the door,

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.541
and you would not sign a
paper without reading it.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.270
The same judgment works online.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.730
Today you will set up your
station so it is comfortable,

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:29.470
spot a scam and check it a safer way,
and protect your devices and files.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:36.250
Then you will show five skills from
this course and take the post-test.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:38.370
Each step starts the same way.

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:43.550
Pause, look closely,
and check before you act.

00:00:48.352 --> 00:00:50.269
Start with your body and your screen.

00:00:51.072 --> 00:00:53.093
Sit so you can read without leaning in.

00:00:53.892 --> 00:00:58.012
The screen should be about an arm's
length away, with the top near eye level.

00:00:59.212 --> 00:01:02.632
If the words are too small,
Magnifier makes everything bigger.

00:01:03.632 --> 00:01:06.812
Press the Windows key and plus to zoom in,
and the Windows

00:01:06.812 --> 00:01:08.652
key and Escape to close it.

00:01:09.892 --> 00:01:11.592
Browser zoom is different.

00:01:12.332 --> 00:01:16.072
It enlarges one website,
and Control and zero puts it back.

00:01:17.492 --> 00:01:19.212
Change one thing at a time,

00:01:19.852 --> 00:01:23.512
notice what got easier, and put
shared settings back before you leave.

00:01:24.552 --> 00:01:28.852
If alerts keep pulling you away,
Do not disturb quiets them while you work.

00:01:30.699 --> 00:01:34.179
Some features help far more
people than you might expect.

00:01:35.419 --> 00:01:37.379
Captions help in a noisy room,

00:01:37.959 --> 00:01:41.919
in a quiet library, and for anyone
who hears less than they used to.

00:01:42.979 --> 00:01:45.959
Keyboard access helps someone
who cannot use a mouse.

00:01:47.299 --> 00:01:51.319
Press Tab, and a visible outline
moves from one control to the next.

00:01:52.339 --> 00:01:53.559
Color is another one.

00:01:54.319 --> 00:01:57.279
If a schedule shows full
shifts only in dark shading,

00:01:57.279 --> 00:02:00.899
some people will miss it,
and so will a black and white printer.

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:03.659
Put a word beside the color.

00:02:04.499 --> 00:02:09.719
Mute this week's video, read its captions,
and press Tab to watch the outline move.

00:02:10.659 --> 00:02:11.679
Pause the video now.

00:02:25.841 --> 00:02:27.401
Here is a fictional message.

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:31.601
The subject says Act now:
account will close.

00:02:32.461 --> 00:02:35.121
It comes from support at
account-check dot example,

00:02:35.601 --> 00:02:38.321
and it asks you to verify
your password immediately.

00:02:40.141 --> 00:02:42.201
Three things should slow you down.

00:02:43.341 --> 00:02:44.999
The message pushes you to hurry.

00:02:45.761 --> 00:02:48.714
The sender does not match any
organization you deal with.

00:02:49.041 --> 00:02:54.141
And it asks for a password, which a real
organization will not request by email.

00:02:55.841 --> 00:02:59.741
A familiar logo proves nothing,
because anyone can copy a logo.

00:03:00.581 --> 00:03:03.586
Spelling can be perfect and
the message can still be fake.

00:03:04.561 --> 00:03:07.358
This kind of fake message
is called phishing.

00:03:08.908 --> 00:03:13.068 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Never use the link or the phone
number inside a suspicious message.

00:03:13.808 --> 00:03:15.888 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Reach the organization another way.

00:03:16.688 --> 00:03:18.148 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Open a bookmark you saved,

00:03:18.768 --> 00:03:22.291 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
type an address you know,
or call the number on your card or letter.

00:03:23.788 --> 00:03:25.828 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Then ask whether the problem is real.

00:03:27.128 --> 00:03:28.941 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
If you already typed your password,

00:03:29.208 --> 00:03:30.208 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
go to the real site,

00:03:30.688 --> 00:03:35.648 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
change it, and turn on two-step
verification, so a code is needed as well.

00:03:36.868 --> 00:03:38.323 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Then tell someone who can help.

00:03:39.548 --> 00:03:41.348 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Open the Mission 5B practice page.

00:03:42.448 --> 00:03:43.387 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Inspect the subject,

00:03:43.748 --> 00:03:48.728 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
the sender, and the link, and write
two warning signs and your safer check.

00:03:50.068 --> 00:03:51.048 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Pause the video now.

00:04:02.165 --> 00:04:03.978
Not every risk arrives as an email.

00:04:05.005 --> 00:04:07.803
A new online friend may be
warm and patient for weeks,

00:04:08.505 --> 00:04:12.505
then ask for money, often gift cards,
and ask you to keep it secret.

00:04:13.945 --> 00:04:18.142
Secrecy and money together are a reason
to stop and check with someone you trust.

00:04:19.505 --> 00:04:23.443
Scrolling late at night because you
might miss something is a real pull too.

00:04:24.125 --> 00:04:26.405
Set a stopping point,
or quiet your alerts.

00:04:27.785 --> 00:04:32.045
If someone posts something hurtful
about you, do not reply in anger.

00:04:32.945 --> 00:04:37.205
Keep a screenshot if it is safe,
report or block, and talk to someone.

00:04:38.625 --> 00:04:41.765
Write a safe next step for each
situation on your worksheet.

00:04:42.725 --> 00:04:43.664
Pause the video now.

00:04:57.632 --> 00:05:01.312 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Updates fix security holes,
so let Windows Update do its job.

00:05:02.972 --> 00:05:06.772 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Home computers still on Windows
10 need Extended Security Updates,

00:05:06.772 --> 00:05:12.272 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
which run through October 2027,
or a move to a supported device.

00:05:13.532 --> 00:05:16.552 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
A website may ask to use
your camera or microphone.

00:05:17.612 --> 00:05:19.270 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Allow it when the task needs it,

00:05:19.292 --> 00:05:24.132 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
such as a video meeting, and block it when
it does not, such as a page you only read.

00:05:25.072 --> 00:05:27.992 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
A permission can stay
on after a call ends,

00:05:28.612 --> 00:05:31.203 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
so check it in your browser
settings now and then.

00:05:31.512 --> 00:05:35.932 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
If you find a USB drive you do
not recognize, do not plug it in.

00:05:36.632 --> 00:05:37.430 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Give it to staff.

00:05:40.606 --> 00:05:42.679
Different protections do different jobs.

00:05:43.226 --> 00:05:44.486
In Word, a password

00:05:44.486 --> 00:05:48.326
to open a file encrypts it, so nobody
can read it without that password.

00:05:48.326 --> 00:05:53.466
Read-only only limits changes;
anyone can still read the words.

00:05:54.906 --> 00:05:58.326
Device encryption protects a laptop
that is lost while turned off.

00:05:59.046 --> 00:06:02.146
None of these protect a
computer left signed in, so

00:06:02.146 --> 00:06:05.926
press Windows and L to lock it,
or sign out of a shared computer.

00:06:06.986 --> 00:06:10.466
For accounts,
use a passkey when a site offers one.

00:06:10.466 --> 00:06:14.726
Otherwise use a long, unique
passphrase with two-step verification.

00:06:16.206 --> 00:06:18.006
On the Mission 5C practice page,

00:06:18.246 --> 00:06:22.443
choose before you flip each card, then
look at the settings your worksheet names.

00:06:23.286 --> 00:06:24.446
Pause the video now.

00:06:38.535 --> 00:06:41.695
Now you get to show what you can do.

00:06:42.995 --> 00:06:46.575
The skills challenge has five tasks,
and you work on your own.

00:06:47.515 --> 00:06:49.775
Ask a search question
that names the place,

00:06:49.775 --> 00:06:52.095
and open the organization's own page.

00:06:53.475 --> 00:06:56.014
Build a short Word paragraph
with a real heading,

00:06:56.555 --> 00:06:58.795
three numbered steps, and a bold warning.

00:06:59.875 --> 00:07:02.635
Total four costs in Excel with a formula.

00:07:03.735 --> 00:07:06.695
Delete a line,
then bring it back with Undo.

00:07:08.075 --> 00:07:10.675
And write useful feedback
on a partner's flyer.

00:07:12.255 --> 00:07:13.476
The quick card is allowed.

00:07:14.715 --> 00:07:17.876
Your instructor watches and
records how much help you needed.

00:07:18.275 --> 00:07:20.244
This is separate from your test score,

00:07:20.255 --> 00:07:22.794
and it shows what you can
already do on your own.

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:30.450
Last comes the post-test.

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:34.510
It has twenty questions, and you take
it on your own, with no coaching.

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:40.850
When you finish, submit your results to
Britt and save the complete graded PDF.

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:45.990
The results show your score
and which areas went well.

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:49.682
Treat them as a learning plan,
not a verdict.

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:55.090
Pick one strength to keep using
and one skill to practice next.

00:07:56.250 --> 00:08:01.050
Then compare where you started on
the pre-test with where you are now.

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:06.650
Every row you completed in this course
is something you can do again at home,

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:08.010
at your own pace.
